From Melania.Pintilie at uhn.on.ca  Mon Feb 28 13:39:25 2000
From: Melania.Pintilie at uhn.on.ca (Pintilie, Melania)
Date: Mon, 28 Feb 2000 08:39:25 -0500
Subject: [S] strange behaviour of coxph - answers
Message-ID: <00Mar6.162332est.619406@gateway.healthlink.on.ca>

On Feb 24 I posted the following question:
I seem to get different results whether coxph is in a function or an
interactive call.

The explanation came from Dr. T. Therneau:
The problem described is not with coxph, but with argument matching.  It
is a common issue.

   The Splus argument matching model is that a function can see data in
the directory list (what search() lists)  plus ONE more place, for the
purpose of matching its arguments.  
   Normally that "one more place" is the parent function that called it,
but when we use a modeling function with a "data=" statement, the one more
place is the named data frame.

  In your example
    junk <- function(i) {
        temp1 <- i
        coxph(Surv..... ~dd[i], data=dd)
        }

That interior coxph call sees data in data frame "dd", but the variable
"temp1"
is invisible.  So is the local variable "i".  You get the result that you do
because there is a variable "i" in your working directory with some value.
If you had used ...dd[temp1] ... in the call you would have seen "temp1 not
found" as a message.

The solution came from Prof. B Ripley:
junk2 <- function(i)
{
        assign("i", i. frame=1)
        fit <- coxph(Surv(dftime, dfcens) ~ dd[, i], data = dd)
        return(i, dd[, i], fit)
}

Since in my function I called cox.zph after coxph the solution which I
foundworked best for me was to create inside the function a data frame which
contains all the necessary variables. I assign it and then do whatever
calculations I need.

Part of the original function is:
pcont_function(i)
{
  survtime_dd$dftime[!is.na(dd[,i])]
  stat_dd$dfcens[!is.na(dd[,i])]
  variab_dd[!is.na(dd[,i]),i]
 
  
  fit_coxph(Surv(survtime,stat)~variab, data=ddtemp)
  schoenr_cox.zph(fit,transform='rank')
}

The final function is:
pcont_function(i)
{
  survtime_dd$dftime[!is.na(dd[,i])]
  stat_dd$dfcens[!is.na(dd[,i])]
  variab_dd[!is.na(dd[,i]),i]
  ddtemp_data.frame(survtime,stat,variab)
  assign('ddtemp',ddtemp,frame=1)
  fit_coxph(Surv(survtime,stat)~variab, data=ddtemp)
  schoenr_cox.zph(fit,transform='rank')
}

Thank you for the quick and helpful hints. Without them, I would have
probably restricted the use of S-plus, since the first time the function did
not return with an error but with WRONG results.

melania

