Thanks to Peter Perkins and Jaimyoung Kwon who responded to my previous MLE
question. Jaimyoung's suggestion is to use "ms" for "burn-in" and getting
the initial values after several iterations, then apply NR method. Peter did
some simulations using "nlminb". Below is his program and results.

#############
> First I tranfered the correlation coefficient p by d =
> log((1-p)/(1+p)), and sigma1 and sigma2 by lsigma1 = 1/sigma1,
> lsigma2=1/sigma2 in the loglikelihood function.

i understand the rho transformation (gets you an unconstrained param,
right?), but i don't follow the ones for the sd's - are they inended to
make the parameter estimates uncorrelated, or to improve convergence?
i'm no expert here.  in any case, even without a gradient:

> sim <- function(n = 1000)
{
   ll <- function(x)
   {
      ll <- sum(log(dmvnorm(y, mean = x[1:2], sd = x[3:4], rho = x[5])))
      #print(signif(c(x, ll), 5))
 - ll
   }
   ll2 <- function(x)
   {
      ll <- sum(log(dmvnorm(y, mean = x[1:2], sd = 1/x[3:4],
                            rho = (1 - exp(x[5]))/(1 + exp(x[5])))))
      #print(signif(c(x, ll), 5))
 - ll
   }
   mu <- c(1, 1)
   sig <- c(1, 1)
   rho <- 0.25
   d <- log((1 - rho)/(1 + rho))
   EPS <- 0.001
   assign("y", rmvnorm(n, mean = mu, sd = sig, rho = rho), frame = 1)
   x <- c(mu = mu, sd = sig, rho = rho)
   control <- nlminb.control(step.max = 0.1)
   rslt <- nlminb(x, ll, control = control, lower = c( - Inf,  - Inf,
                EPS, EPS, EPS - 1), upper = c(Inf, Inf, Inf, Inf, 1 -
EPS))
   cat("no transforms:\n")
   print(rslt)
   #
   siginv <- 1/sig
   d <- log((1 - rho)/(1 + rho))
   x <- c(mu = mu, sdinv = siginv, d = d)
   rslt <- nlminb(x, ll2, control = control, lower = c( - Inf,  - Inf,
                EPS, EPS,  - Inf), upper = c(Inf, Inf, Inf, Inf, Inf))
   cat("transformed:\n")
   print(rslt)
   invisible()
}

