Subject: [S] polar smoothing summary
Message-ID: <s8c7825c.028@mail2.epa.state.oh.us>

Thanks to Nick Ellis and Paul Eilers for their help on this one. 

My original question (appended below) was : how to perform a circular smooth 
from a linear fit within polar coordinates? The trick is in "unfolding the 
data". As it happens, I just couldnt follow Cleveland's outline on this part. 

Many thanks !

********************************
Reply from Nick Ellis:

The lm version (which simply fits a straight line) is not continuous across
the theta jump - you wouldn't expect a global model to be so. Even the loess
model isn't quite continuous, but it will become more so if you increase the
number of unfoldings and the number of knots. A model that ensures
continuity is one that uses sine and cosine bases, as included below.

y.unfold <- c(z1,z1,z1) # z1 are the radii of the transformed (to polar coords) 
data
thet.unfold <- c(thet-2*pi,thet,thet+2*pi) # thet are the associated angles
z.fit <-lm(y.unfold~sin(thet.unfold)+cos(thet.unfold)+sin(2*thet.unfold)+
cos(2*thet.unfold))plot(thet.unfold,y.unfold)

Nick Ellis
