Thanks a lot to Rolf Turner, Angelo J. Canty, Bill Dunlap , Sam Buttrey,

Jose' Pinheiro, Michael Ernst, Prof Brian D. Ripley, Andy Liaw, for
their very quick reply to my question regarding the use of  "sample".

Here are the responses which allowed me to solve the problem. They are
basically the same :

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Sam Buttrey:

Hi. Try this.

u.herds <- unique (surf4[,"herd"]) # get list of unique herd numbers

your.sample <- sample (u.herds, sample.size) # put in your "sample size"

here

surftest <- surf4[as.logical (match (surf4[,"herd"], your.sample, 0)),]

The "match" function produces a vector with length equal to nrow(surf).
Each
element tells where, in "your.sample", the corresponding element of
surf4[,"herd"] appears (the third argument says "return a 0 if there's
no
match"). By converting that to a logical, we get a vector of T's and
F's,
and when we extract the rows of the data frame for which there's a T in
that
vector, we're finished.
I hope this helps,
Bonne chance,
