From therneau at mayo.edu  Mon Mar 13 05:05:52 2000
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Therneau, Terry M., Ph.D.)
Date: Sun, 12 Mar 2000 23:05:52 -0600
Subject: [S] Multiple frailty terms
Message-ID: <200003130505.XAA01863@rocky.Mayo.edu>

  Ramon Duaz-Uriarte asks "how to fit multiple random effects" in a 
Cox model.  There is a way, albeit imperfect.

 The frailty models in S-Plus have serious limitations (I should know, I
wrote the programs).  What they do, they do well.  But they don't do nearly
all that I'd like.  Some important design questions about how they
should be extended remain open.

1. Multiple effects:  The architecture of the penalized models was
built for extensibility, i.e., it's very easy to add new types of
penalty terms.  One unfortunate consequence of this is that each 
penalized term is under control of it's own maximization routine, independent
of the others.  This works ok if terms are essentially fixed, e.g.,

        coxph(Surv(time, status) ~ age + frailty(city, theta=.5) +
                                       + frailty(school, theta=.4), data=mine)

is good.  It even works ok when "df" replaces "theta" as the constraint.
But if both frailty terms are free, the minimization routines just plain get
lost.  (The minimizer for the first frailty term assumes that its parameter
is the only one varying, ditto the second).  The exact same thing happens
if more than one pspline term with "df=0 or method=aic" is
included in a model.

   A solution is to imbed the coxph function as the object of a more
general search, i.e.,
        tfun <- function(par) {
          fit <- coxph(Surv(time, status) ~ age + frailty(city, theta=par[1])
                              + frailty(school, theta=par[2]))
          2 * (diff(fit$logik) - sum(fit$df))
          }
which returns the AIC for the model with given frailty variances.  (You
need to attach() the data set instead of using data= in the model -- the
subject of many an S news discussion and not specific to coxph).  The
function tfun can then be maximized using one of the general maximizers
such as nlminb.

  Please note: the equivalence of a gamma frailty to penalized regression
holds ONLY for a single random term; you have to use AIC rather than the
likelihood based criteria for choosing theta when there is more than one term.

2. Continuous variables.  The frailty(x) function assumes that x is a 
factor.  In SAS terms, we only allow CLASS variables.

3. Correlated random effects.  This is in the works, but don't hold
your breath.  Suffice it to say that the numerical efficiency aspects of
this have proven to be quite challenging.  We also need to use the model
for some data within the next year, so it will get done.

4. Survival curves and/or residuals after the fit of a frailty model.
The basic functionality is there, but worked examples are needed along
with some code fixes.  (I've recently discovered some logic mistakes; all
lead to `I am confused' messages from the code and no answers, when it's
really ok to proceed).  

5. A fundamental design issue:  I need to create a roadmap of what the
random effects Cox/survreg models `should' be able to do.  
What additions/changes to the model formula will be needed to specify
these?  How much syntax should be borrowed from lme and nlme?  
Comments on what it should do are welcome.

