From xgsu at wald.ucdavis.edu  Mon Mar 13 21:34:35 2000
From: xgsu at wald.ucdavis.edu (Xiao Gang Su)
Date: Mon, 13 Mar 2000 13:34:35 -0800
Subject: Re: [S] bivariate gamma
In-Reply-To: <HCCMJCJECAJDDAAA@mailcity.com>
References: <HCCMJCJECAJDDAAA@mailcity.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.OSF.4.05.10003131306330.18295-100000@macro.ucdavis.edu>

Hi, there,

One nice property about gamma that I believe you might be intersted is
that:

if X1, ..., Xk are independent and if Xi ~ Gamma(ai, b) (i=1,...,k), then
the sum X1+ ... + Xk has a gamma distribution with parameter (a1+...aK,
b).

Therefore, if you want to generate bivaraite correlated gamma
distribution, you could set 
    Y1 = (X1+...+XK) + (Z1+...+Zm)
    Y2 = (X1+...+XK) + (C1+...+Cm)
where x1, ..., Xk, Z1, ..., Zm, C1, ..., Cm are all independent gammma
random varaibles. Hence Y1 and Y2 are identically gamma distributed. The
correlation between Y1 and Y2 is induced through (X1 + ..., XK).
This correlation can be computed either theoretically, or through
simulations if their joint distribution function is too complicated. 
The nice thing about this way of simulating is that you can change their
correlation easily by adjusting the rate K/m. 
  

Xiaogang

