Hello all,

I have a number of Cox proportional hazards models and would like
to use the update command to modify the formulas in an automated
fashion.  What I want to do could be hacked together with string
manipulations and the as.formula command, but I was hoping a more
elegant way was possible using update.formula.  For the curious,
I'm trying to calculate likelihood ratio confidence intervals for
each of the covariates.

Specifically, I would like to do something like the following:

######
mymodel <- coxph(Surv(start,stop,event)~age+weight+height,...)

myformula <- mymodel$formula
varlist <- all.vars(myformula)

for (i in 1:varlist) {

  newformula <-update(myformula,~.-varlist[i]+offset(beta*varlist[i]))
  newmodel <- coxph(formula=newformula,...)

#  more operations on the newmodel here...

}
########

As you can guess, this does not work.  The update command does
not realize that the contents of varlist[i] is a character string
of the name of a variable that I would like to modify in
myformula.  For example, if varlist[1]="age", then the update
command with i=1 would give the formula

Surv(start,stop,event)~age+height+weight+offset(beta*varlist[i])

I was hoping there was a command (or two) which I could wrap
around varlist in the update call, say eval(varlist[i]) or some
such, which would fix this.  Nothing I've tried has worked so
far.

Any suggestions would be appreciated.  

