From robertl at ccbr.umn.edu  Wed Mar 15 15:39:57 2000
From: robertl at ccbr.umn.edu (Robert Leduc)
Date: Wed, 15 Mar 2000 09:39:57 -0600
Subject: [S] Summary of Responses to "update.formula"
Message-ID: <Pine.SUN.4.21.0003150924420.2326-100000@cricket.ccbr.umn.edu>

Hello all,

Thanks to Brian Ripley and Patrick Connolly for their responses.

The original problem can briefly be described as needing to
update a formula object by deleting one covariate and adding an
offset.  This could easily be done with the update command if one
wanted to "hard code" the names of the particular covariates.  
Because there were a number of covariates (and a number of
models) it is attractive to try to do this generically, i.e.
without reference to the specific formula or covariate names.  
My particular application is to compute likelihood ratio
confidence intervals for each covariate in the model.

Both Brian and Patrick suggested variants of using "paste" to
construct a new formula.  Brian also suggested the use of the
"substitute" command.  My ignorance has been a barrier to
implementing that solution.  The paste solution works quite well
as follows:

Suppose the formula is in a formula object, e.g.

my.formula <- ~age+height+weight   #  actually extracted from a
                                   #  coxph object in my code.

One could then loop through each covariate by setting tmp to each
covariate name in turn:

tmp <- "age"  # e.g.

The magic command that does it all:

new.formula <- eval(parse(text=
     paste("update(my.formula,","~.-",tmp,"+offset(beta*",tmp,"))",sep="")))

Here beta is a constant.  After executing this command, new.formula should
contain the formula

      ~height+weight+offset(beta*age)

It is instructive for beginners such as myself to interactively execute
this command from the inside out, i.e. first do the paste, then the parse 
of the .Last.result, then the eval of that result.

One can then generate a new model with this formula by

new.model <- coxph(formula=new.formula,blahblahblah).

Thanks again to Brian and Patrick!

Rob Leduc
Division of Biostatistics
University of Minnesota

