From SorkinJ at grc.nia.nih.gov  Wed Mar 15 22:09:38 2000
From: SorkinJ at grc.nia.nih.gov (Sorkin, John)
Date: Wed, 15 Mar 2000 17:09:38 -0500
Subject: [S] Summary: Please help me understand (psuedo) random number generation
Message-ID: <C5BE6D142223D211909E0000F8CD228A7C1F7F@c3.grc.nia.nih.gov>

Re: Please help me understand (psuedo) random number generation

I must thank the SPlus users who rapidly assisted me with my question. In
particular, I would like to thank Peter Perkins, Scott.Chasalow, Kevin Brand,
David Evans, Tom Burr, Patrick Connolly, and Jim Stapleton. If I have neglected
to thank anyone else it is only because I have not yet received your message. 

As to my first question, why are the means of the random vectors not closer to
zero:

Please remember that each vector consisted of 5,000 elements. Also remember that
the call to menuRandomNumbers had in it sd=10. [I would have gotten the same
results if I had used the more familiar function rnorm(5000, mean = 0, sd =
10)]. I expected the data SERIES returned from menuRandomNumbers (or rnorm) to
have a mean of 0 and an SD of 10. As several people have pointed out, it is not
the SERIES that has a mean of zero and an SD of 10, but rather each of the 5,000
elements is drawn from a DISTRIBUTION whose mean is 0 and whose SD is 10. Thus
each of the 5,000 elements can be looked at as if each one represents the mean
of a sample of values, each mean having an SD of 10. As we know from elementary
statistics, the variance of 5,000 objects, each of which has a variance of x is
not x but rather
      x/n.
In my case because the SD of each of the 5000 samples was 10, the variance of
each sample is 10^2 or 100. Thus x is 10 and the variance of the series of 5000
samples should be 
      SD^2/5000= 10^2/5000= 100/5000= 0.02 .
Converting the variance of 0.02 back to an SD we have
      SD=Sqrt(var)= sqrt(0.02)= 0.14 .
This means that 95% of the time we would expect the mean of the series of 5,000
estimates to range from 
mean-1.96*0.14=mean-0.28 to
mean+1.96*0.14=mean+0.28.
Now if the true mean were 0, we would expect the mean of the 5,000 elements to
range from
0-1.96*0.14=-0.28 to 0+1.96*0.14=+0.28.

The mean of my first series, errorone, was 0.288 (very slightly larger than
expected) the mean of the second series, errortwo, was 0.10 which is well within
the 95% CI.

Therefore, my concern about the means of the series was unfounded; it was based
on an unreasonable "expectation". (I could not resist the pun . . .)    

As to my second question, why the summary statistics printed twice. Although I
do not quite understand the logic behind the solution, the solution is simply to
put 
invisible() 
immediately after the menuDescribe function. Apparently menuDescribe is some
sort of "printing" function. 

Thanks again to everyone who helped me.

John Sorkin

P.S. 

In order to complete the thread that began with my original posting in one
piece, I am appending my original posting to the end of this message.

ORIGINAL POSTING:

Colleagues,

I have written a function (see below) that defines two random vectors which are
part of the object rdat. Each of the random vectors should have a mean of ZERO,
and a standard deviation of  10.  I am a little surprised to see that the means
of the random vectors are not ZERO, but rather 0.29 and 0.10. Similarly the SD
of the first vector is 9.7, not 10. I do not believe the problem is sample size;
you will note that each random vector has 5,000 entries. Could some one please
explain why my means are not closer to zero, and why one of the SDs is not
closer to ten? Have I made a mistake? Is my problem related to the seed value
that I used in constructing the random vectors? Have I encountered a limitation
with a pseudo random number generator?

Thanks,
