The S-Plus courses that MathSoft and I are offering in May and August (see 
http://www.splus.mathsoft.com/splus/train/2000schedule.htm) have generated some 
questions which I would like to answer.

The 2-day course "S-PLUS for Statistical Data Analysis and Graphics"  is 2/3 to 
3/4 S-Plus command-oriented (relying heavily on the Hmisc library - see 
http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/Hmisc.html) and the remainder uses 
the graphical user interface, demonstrated on S-Plus 2000.  Roughly 1/3 of this 
course involves hands-on computer lab exercises.  The functions in the Hmisc 
library that will be emphasized include xYplot, Dotplot, summary.formula, ecdf, 
datadensity, summarize, describe, panel.bpplot, and cleanup.import.  Some of 
the sample size and power functions will also be discussed.

The course spends about 20 minutes on LaTeX, a technical document/report/book 
processing system available for free on virtually all platforms including 
Windows 95/98/NT (see http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/EmacsTeX/ and 
the report on making statistical reports found under the Hmisc web page).  The 
course will touch on making HTML tables from S-Plus, for  non-LaTeX users.  You 
can examine the book by Alzola & Harrell to get a good feel for the course (see 
the Hmisc web page; this book will be updated bofore the course and a copy will 
be provided for all participants at the course).  The course will also briefly 
cover some of the principles of good graphics found in Bill Cleveland's 
excellent book "The Elements of Graphing Data".

The 3-day course "Regression Modeling Strategies" is all command-oriented, 
using the Design library (see 
http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/Design.html).  In the course 
description you will see recommended pre-course readings.  If you register for 
the course and you do not have access to a library that has Statistics in 
Medicine or JAMA, please E-mail me and I'll mail reprints of these articles to 
you.  Note that although these articles contain only medical examples, the 
methods they used are not specific to medical research.  The two articles in 
Statistics in Medicine contain all of the S-Plus code used to do the analyses.

A litle over 1/3 of the 3-day course is hands-on laboratory exercises.
