From mcdonr at rpi.edu  Thu Mar 16 18:18:28 2000
From: mcdonr at rpi.edu (Robert McDonald)
Date: Thu, 16 Mar 2000 13:18:28 -0500
Subject: [S] Mime-Version: 1.0
Message-ID: <4.2.0.58.20000316131609.0097a200@mail.rpi.edu>

Hi,

I am trying to program a Monte Carlo simulation. Because I am a novice user 
of SPLUS2000 for windows and a very poor programmer, I am having difficulty 
programming loops.

I have generated data that is contained in a dataframe. There are three 
vectors in the dataframe: rep, y1, and y2. rep indicates the replication 
number (i.e., sample number), y1 and y2 contain the data. For example:

rep     y1       y2
1        530     535
1        550     565
1        560     545
1        535     475
2        595     560
2        460     455
2        575     550
2        615     490

I want to calculate the 95% confidence intervals for comparing two 
dependent groups on the median using Rand Wilcox's SPLUS function (page 127 
of his book) for each of the two replications. This function returns the 
lower and upper ci in a list. I want the confidence intervals for each 
replication to be put into a dataset with three vectors: rep, lower ci, 
upper ci. In reality, there will be many replications (perhaps 2,000) and 
the size of each replication could be as high as 150 observations.

The Wilcox function has the form:

	bootdpci (y1, y2, fun, nboot, alpha)

If it helps, I put the definition for the function below.

I tried lapply, but it calculates a function on a column by column basis. I 
also unsuccessfully tried aggregate and several others. Thanks in advance 
and sorry for the inconvenience,

Rob McDonald
Lally School of Management and Technology
Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute

Reference: Wilcox, R.R. (1997). Introduction to robust estimation and 
hypothesis testing. Academic Press.

bootdpci
function(x, y, fun = onestep, nboot = 599, alpha = 0.05)
{
#
# Using the percentile bootstrap method,
# compute a .95 confidence interval for the difference between
# a measure of location or scale
# when comparing two dependent groups.
# By default, a one-step M-estimator (with Huber's psi) is used.
# If, for example, it is desired to use a fully iterated
# M-estimator, use fun=mest when calling this function.
#
if(length(x) != length(y)) stop(
"Must have equal sample sizes.")
if(sum(c(!is.na(x), !is.na(y))) != (length(x) + length(y))
)
stop("Missing values are not allowed.")
set.seed(2) # set seed of random number generator so tha
t
# results can be duplicated.
print("Taking bootstrap samples. Please wait.")
data <- matrix(sample(length(y), size = length(y) * nboot,
replace = T), nrow = nboot)
boot1 <- matrix(x[data], nrow = nboot, ncol = length(y))
bvec1 <- apply(boot1, 1, fun)
# A 1 by nboot matrix containing the bootstrap
# estimates for the first group
boot2 <- matrix(y[data], nrow = nboot, ncol = length(y))
bvec2 <- apply(boot2, 1, fun)
# A 1 by nboot matrix containing the bootstrap
# estimates for the second group.
bvec <- bvec1 - bvec2
ihi <- floor((1 - alpha/2) * nboot + 0.5)
ilow <- floor((alpha/2) * nboot + 0.5)
bsort <- sort(bvec)
ci <- 0
ci[1] <- bsort[ilow]
ci[2] <- bsort[ihi]
list(ci = ci)
}

