>>From deviance you can test hypotheses about effects but deviance is not so 
>good for assessing goodness of fit, in the sense that it is not good for 
>assessing lack of fit (R-square shares this problem).  See

@ARTICLE{hos97com,
  author = {Hosmer, D. W. and Hosmer, T. and {le Cessie}, S. and Lemeshow, S.},
  year = 1997,
  title = {A comparison of goodness-of-fit tests for the logistic regression
          model},
  journal = Stat in Medicine,
  volume = 16,
  pages = {965-980},
  annote = {goodness-of-fit for binary logistic model;difficulty with
           Hosmer-Lemeshow statistic being dependent on how groups are
           defined;sum of squares test;cumulative sum test;invalidity of naive
           test based on deviance;goodness-of-link function;simulation setup}
}

The test advocated in the Hosmer article is the sum of squared residuals test, 
a one degree of freedom omnibus test for lack of fit for a binary logistic 
model.  This test is implemented with the lrm function in the S-Plus Design 
library (see residuals.lrm).  Note: If that's all you need, it's probably not 
worth installing the Design library.]
No one has implemented this for probit models but check the above paper's 
description of goodness-of-link tests for choosing between logit and probit.

-Frank

