On Fri, 17 Mar 2000, Frank E Harrell Jr wrote:

> A few years ago I got tired of dealing with these problems so in the 
> Hmisc library I redefined [.factor to, by default, remove unused levels.  
> That way you don't need to say drop=T everywhere.  The user overrides 
> this with a global system option (options(drop.unused.levels=F)).  -Frank

I don't believe that is the right answer. Properly written software should
understand that factors can have absent levels. Dropping them removes
information, in some problems (e.g. classification) important information.
There's something of a history here of damaging the original correct design
to cope with poorly implemented usages.  In this case, whyever does
the GUI function not cope?

There is also a quite serious problem in changing the behaviour of the
system to suit a particular extension.  That change breaks quite a lot
of other people's code.  Beware!

