Brian Ripley wrote --
> I don't believe that is the right answer. Properly written software should
> understand that factors can have absent levels. Dropping them removes
> information, in some problems (e.g. classification) important information.
> There's something of a history here of damaging the original correct design
> to cope with poorly implemented usages. 

  I have to respectfully but vehemently disagree with "original correct
design", for something that generates so much grief, including weekly
pleas to the news group.  At Mayo, we have changed system options so as
to NEVER generate factors unless the user specifically asks for one with
"factor(....)".  This, along with globally setting na.action=na.omit, make
the package usable.  
  Unfortunately, doing so requires version-specific and undocumented code.
For 3.4 "assign("as.data.frame.character", as.data.frame.vector, frame=0)
plus a replacement of sas.get() was required, for 5.1 we have to replace
data.frameAux as well.  (We don't use ImportData - likely need to replace
that too).  A system option "auto create factors" is sorely needed.  It can
default to T, just as long as our group can reset it to the more practical
value.

        Terry Therneau

