From fharrell at virginia.edu  Fri Mar 17 13:14:13 2000
From: fharrell at virginia.edu (Frank E Harrell Jr)
Date: Fri, 17 Mar 2000 08:14:13 -0500
Subject: Re: [S] Logit - Probit interpretation
References: <Pine.GSO.4.05.10003171126070.26329-100000@auk.stats>
Message-ID: <38D22FA4.C3D09101@virginia.edu>

Brian - I hope you don't mind that I cc'd this to s-news as I think others will 
be interested.

Prof Brian D Ripley wrote:

> On Fri, 17 Mar 2000, Frank E Harrell Jr wrote:
>
> >
> > >From deviance you can test hypotheses about effects but deviance is
> > not so good for assessing goodness of fit, in the sense that it is not
> > good for assessing lack of fit (R-square shares this problem).  See
>
> Eh?  The residual deviance is not good for statistical testing of lack of
> fit, but in a logistic regression it is just log-probability scoring, by
> far the most supported method for assessing probabliity forecasts.  There
> are two issues: is the value returned a good measure of fit (I'd say yes)
> and is there robust distribution theory for it as a test statistic (which
> as Frank addresses, is not so).  Statisticians seem to muddle the two.

Agreed.  Log probability scores, like quadratic probability scores are very 
useful measures that combine calibration accuracy with discrimination ability.  
I, perhaps unlike many, tend to think of goodness of fit as the complement of 
lack of fit.  That's why I don't find the term "goodness of fit" particularly 
helpful in some contexts.

>
>
> I am not at all sure that we should be interested in a portmanteau
> test of lack of fit, anyway.  Models can easily pass such as test yet be
> much inferior to alternative models or have serious biases.
> (It all depends what you are using logistic regression for. If you want to
> predict if the posterior probability is less than 10%, for example
> in an SGA study, only certain very small aspects of the goodness of fit are
> relevant.)

The 1 d.f. sum of squared residuals test is principally a replacement for the 
multiple degree of freedom Hosmer-Lemeshow test, as it has more power "on the 
average" and does not depend on the choice of any cutpoints in predicted 
probabilities.  But as you said, directed hypothesis tests (e.g., tests of 
linearity, interaction) are more powerful.  They also lead you directly to 
corrective action.

.......

-Frank

