R and S-Plus are two different things, although R and S-Plus 3.x (or, S version
3) are very similar in some ways and much software is easily portable between
them.

For R, see http://lib.stat.cmu.edu/R/CRAN/, which has links to archive
sites, etc.  This is truly free in the best spirit of cooperatively developed
freeware.

Despite some confusing information that has emanated from Mathsoft, there
is no such thing as a free version of S-Plus for students.  Somebody pays
for the license at the university, and they may then decide to distribute
it free to students of the university.  I think this is a good deal for
the students (and for the university as well if they use S-Plus and want
to be able to use it in instruction and student research) -- which is why
I allowed my name to be used in the advertising -- but it IS NOT FREE!

