From Bill.Venables at cmis.csiro.au  Fri Mar 17 21:37:05 2000
From: Bill.Venables at cmis.csiro.au (Bill Venables)
Date: Sat, 18 Mar 2000 07:37:05 +1000
Subject: Re: [S] Simulating a multiple logistic regression
Message-ID: <3.0.32.20000318073703.0124b210@pophost.nsw.cmis.csiro.au>

At 01:55 PM 17/3/2000 -0500, Frank E Harrell Jr wrote:
>
> It's not too helpful to think in terms of residuals (which would not 
> be a binomial distribution anyway).  Do something like:
>
> n <- 500
> x1 <- rnorm(n)
> x2 <- runif(n)
> logit <-  -1.5 + x1+2*x2
> y <- ifelse(runif(n) < plogis(logit), 1, 0)
>
> -Frank Harrell
 
All correct, but it might be worth noting that the rbinom() function
is vectorized with respect to all arguments, so instead of Frank's last
line using ifelse(), one could use

y <- rbinom(n, size = 1, prob = plogis(logit))

This is the easy one to generalize if you are dealing with non-unit or
variable numbers of trials.

Bill Venables.
