> 
> Monday, March 20, 2000, 9:53:20 PM, you wrote:
> 
> 
> 
> > A while back, someone wanted to know how to get the interim results from an
> > interrupted loop.  Specifically, I am looping a large cross-validation and
> > would like to retrieve all of the results, even if one of the estimations
> > fails to converge.  (right now if this happens, I lose everything).  I
> > apologize ahead of time for this being a repeat.
> >
> > Elaine

We should be clear about not only the fact that Junni's "obvious"
solution will NOT work, but why.  I think this is the greatest
source of confusion about loops in the language.  The fact is,
like all responsible data anlaysis languages S allows for
"backout", that is, it collects together all results from a
single top-level expression (and a for-loop counts as one
expression) and waits for confirmation that the computation is
error-free before it commits anything.   That is, S allows you
some safety margin while you are pointing the gun at your foot.

Of course compiled languages don't do this because you are
expected to do the "backout" yourself before you do anything for
real.  Interactive languages clearly should do this and if S were
to drop this requirement its loops could be made very slick
indeed (and some competitors for S do in fact do this, leading to
some rather ignorant and unfair comparisons being made).

If you are willing to take the plunge, though, how can you
override this safety measure and save partial results?  I can
think of three different strategies, but I am sure there are
more.

1. Use the try() function to execute the expressions within the
loop likely to lead to failure in a controlled environment and
step over the ones, if any, that do fail, but you do build up
memory usage for backout from errors that are not caught this
way.

2. Use assign(....., immediate = T) instead of an ordinary "<-"
assignment within the loop.  This stops if you hit a snag, but
you do save everything up to that point.

3. Use a For() loop instead of a for() loop.  Again, this stops
if you hit a snag, but if the calculation is large is can save
memory, as well as saving partial results.

On this third point, not many people seem to understand just what
a For() loop is, how it works and why it is *sometimes*
advantageous, so perhaps a few words might be useful.

If instead of using a for() loop you were to type each step of
the loop as a separate top-level expression in an S-PLUS session
the computation would do its commitments separately after each
successful step and not have to hold everything in memory until
the last step for backout purposes and thus save on memory
overhead, possibly considerably.

You might think you could do this by somehow unrolling the loop
into an ascii file and source()-ing the file, but this does not
work since source(), too, hoards up its results for backout
purposes. In fact long source() files are a real memory killer,
in general. At this point you might start to think that S needs a
label-and-goto facility but that particularly grotesque language
anti-structure has very wisely not been provided in S, either.

The For() loop is provided for precisely this purpose and there
is really no simple way of emulating it otherwise (other than by
dextrous use of a text editor and an Splus BATCH session).  It
allows you to unroll a loop into an ascii file and execute it as
a collection of top-level expressions in a separate S-PLUS
process, as if you had typed the unrolled loop out by hand and
fed it piece by piece to the evaluator.  In fact it you execute
the For() loop with exec=F it does not do the loop but rather
produces the file for you to do it later using Splus BATCH (or
SPLUS /BATCH) in the same working directory.  The file itself is
quite readible and you can check the stratgey for yourself.  For
it to be effective, though, the overhead in launching a second
S-PLUS process has to be outweighed by the gain in memory
efficiency from ditching backout; it is not always the case, of
course.

Bill Venables.
