From sroberts at picr.man.ac.uk  Thu Mar 23 10:47:46 2000
From: sroberts at picr.man.ac.uk (Steve Roberts)
Date: Thu, 23 Mar 2000 10:47:46 +0000
Subject: [S] Non-linear Binomial models with overdispersion
Message-ID: <c=US%a=_%p=Paterson_Institu%l=LANCELOT-000323104746Z-8411@lancelot.picr.man.ac.uk>

Greetings,

I am trying to fit some non-linear dose-response curves to binary data.
Doing the brute-force direct maximisation of the Binomial likelihood I
find the data is very overdispersed. Has anybody got a good way of
handling this?

My thoughts so far (none of which I have got implementations for in S)

1) Model the overdisperion as a random effect, numerically integrate the
likelihood function to get a marginal likelihood and maximise this. It
is not clear how the hypothesis testing would proceed from then on. 

2) Use something like NLME - but this assumes Normal errors not
Binomial? Weighting using the observed values would give an approximate
answer.

3) Knock up a non-linear version of the Quasi-likelihood approach.
Should be easy as a direct maximisation of the quasi-likelihood. I've
done this before in S for linear models. 

4) Use the Williams procedure - I have implementaions in GLIM for linear
models but not in S for non-linear models. Supposedly equivalent to
Quasi-likelihood - at least for linear models.

5) Use a Iteratively Weighted Least Squares approach and F-tests based
on the residual SS for testing. Anybody have an S-plus implementation of
IWLS for non-linear models?

The books all seem to be saying that the statistical properties of these
models are rather uncertain, but maybe more has been done in the last 10
years that I have not found?

Any comments, suggestions and pointers to more recent literature would
be welcome!

Steve.

