On Wed, 22 Mar 2000, [iso-8859-1] "G?rald Jean" wrote:

> Samuel Buttrey:  ttt <- ttt[sapply (ttt, length) > 0]
>
> Matt Calder, Julian Taylor and Nick Ellis: ttt[!sapply(ttt, is.null)]

Note that these may give different results.  An Splus NULL is a zero long
vector of mode "NULL" and is often used as a return value where a more concrete
mode wouldn't be appropriate.  You can also have zero long vectors of numbers
(or characters, etc.).  The first will weed out all zero long vectors while
the second will just weed out the subset of those which have mode NULL.

