From therneau at mayo.edu  Thu Mar 23 15:20:51 2000
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Therneau, Terry M., Ph.D.)
Date: Thu, 23 Mar 2000 09:20:51 -0600
Subject: Re: [S] Multiple frailty terms?
Message-ID: <200003231520.JAA19206@rocky.Mayo.edu>

  There is special numerical analysis methodology in the frailty code,
specifically to handle the case of a large number of groups, e.g., a
familial study I'm working on where the "frailty" term has 4423 levels.
Since a (4423 + # other variables) squared Hessian matrix would take 
nearly forever to invert in the NR step, a sparse approximation is used.
The bookkeeping for more than 1 sparse term would be atrocious, and the
code doesn't handle it.

   Geir ran into a design oversight on my part:
        1. Term by term, the program decides if "sparse" should be used.
        2. After deciding, it complains if more than 1 term turned out to
request sparse computation!

  Short term solution: set "sparse=F" for all but one term.  Leave the one
with the largest number of levels as is.

   Long term solution: I fix this rather silly mistake.  Due to lag times, you
won't see the solution in distributed code for a while, however.

        Terry Therneau

