Subject: [S] Summary: variance of regression coefficients from arima.mle()
In-Reply-To: <3.0.6.32.20000320200216.00932190@popserver.sfu.ca>
References: <3.0.6.32.20000320200216.00932190@popserver.sfu.ca>
Message-ID: <3.0.6.32.20000323100217.0093fba0@popserver.sfu.ca>

A few days ago, I posted a question regarding the varaince of regression
coefficients for a regression with autocorrelated errors (see original
posting below). I received one response from Samuel Buttrey suggesting the
use of the parametric bootstrap. 

I have since written a function AReg() to compute conditional or exact ML
estimates of the parameters and their variance in one relatively simple
situation, i.e. in the case of a simple linear regression with AR(1) errors
(autoregressive coef., intecept, slope, and variance). 

The function provides asymptotic variances based on the Hessian, as well as
parametric bootstrap estimates of variance. Because the ML estimates of
both the AR parameter and the variance can have severe negative bias, the
function also computes bias-corrected estimates that seem to be
approximately unbiased based on limited simulations. 

Simulations also indicated that the confidence intervals based on
asymptotic variances are too narrow for small sample sizes (n=20 to n=50),
whereas  bootstrap confidence intervals provided actual coverage close to
the specified confidence level.

I would be happy to make the function available to anyone who is interested

Franz

