Hello again, S-newsers,

This is my first cut at mixed-effects models, and thanks to numerous folks
here and elsewhere, I think I'm starting to get the hang of it.  I'm using
S-plus 2000 on Windows95.  My data consists of 15 subjects with 7 or 8
observations on each subject (one observation per year) for a total of 110
observations (I use the na.action = na.omit to take care of the missing
values).  

"Subject" is actually a geographic area for which I have hunter success
rates (normalized using the modified arcsine transformation of Anscombe
(1948)) and a wide range of variables describing the geographic area (% area
forestland, % area rangeland, average slope, st. dev. slope, etc).

The final model I've arrived at has 30 main effects and 20 pairwise
interactions in the fixed portion of the model (the random component is just
~1|Subject).  This feels like an astronomically huge number of parameters
given my sample size.  However, based on anova(finalmodel, test = "F", type
= "marginal"), all parameters are significant at the 0.05 level (even the
interaction terms, and most are significant at the 0.01 level).  Even
variables that are highly correlated (such as % land in private ownership
and % land in public ownership...the only things left out are waterbodies)
are highly significant (p <.0001).  An AR1 autocorrelation is also
significant at p < .0001 (L. Ratio of 36.088 between the model without and
with autocorrelation).  The various diagnostic plots look good.

However, when I try to test for the significance of separate variances for
each Subject by including "weights = varIdent(,~1|Subject)" in the lme()
command, I get the error:

Error in solve.qr(a): apparently singular matrix
Warning messages:
  FALSE CONVERGENCE. in: ms( ~  - logLik(lmeSt, lmePars), start =
list(lmePars = c(coef(lmeSt))),  ...

This occurs whether or not I have the AR1 autocorrelations in the model.
And when I try and get confidence intervals using the Windows front end for
the lme command, I get the error message:

 FALSE CONVERGENCE. in: ms( ~  - logLik(lmeSt, lmePars), start =
list(lmePars = c(coef(lmeSt))),  ...
Error in intervals.lme(obj): Cannot get confidence intervals on var-cov
components: Non-positive definite approximate variance-covariance 

My intuition tells me that this is a sign of an over-parameterized model.
Is it possible that anova tests with type = "marginal" and AIC values have
lead me awry?  Could the fact that I have some extremely strong correlations
in the parameters (even though they're significant) be causing these errors?
Is this something I should even be worried about?

Thank you in advance for any help you might be able to provide!

Sincerely,
Andy
