I am seeking the help of this list as I set out to program the analysis of
cross-sectional structure for a sample of time series observations.  (The
actual data is security returns over time.)  I would like perform the same
linear model estimation on each of about 5000 time series, and collect the
estimated coefficients and other statistics for subsequent analysis. In the
case of a linear regression, the model would regress the time series of
returns for each of the 5000 dependent on the time series data for about 15
independent variables (the same 15 independent variables for all 5000
regressions).  The goal at that stage is to build the (5000 x 16) array of
coefficients and a similar array of t-stats and vectors of other diagnostic
stats.

A variation of this estimation is done in stages, with the first stage being
used to remove some factor components from the time series and the residuals
of the first stage being used as the dependent variable in subsequent
estimation steps.

Yet another variation would have some of the independent variables in the
estimation determined by a table lookup which would depend on the identity
of the specific dependent variable.

I can see how to do this by initializing the result arrays, then using a
"for" loop to cycle through the dependent variables storing results as the
loop loops.  I would hope that there is a more efficient way.  Suggestions?
Are there particular memory management gotchas to consider? Does efficiency
matter when the estimation itself represents the bulk of computation?

Thanks in advance for any suggestions.

