From v_bill_pikounis at merck.com  Wed Mar 29 17:13:56 2000
From: v_bill_pikounis at merck.com (Pikounis, V. Bill)
Date: Wed, 29 Mar 2000 12:13:56 -0500
Subject: RE: [S] S+ vs. SAS
Message-ID: <994DC7B72E4CD311A56D0008C707B35901942C6E@usrymx05.merck.com>

Ming-Yi,

I cannot give simple answers to your questions, but I cannot resist trying
to provide some helpful directions / pointers.  Two more caveats:  I work at
a large pharmaceutical company but NOT in the area of clinical trials; and
my opinions are most certainly NOT reflective of my colleagues (pre-clinical
and clinical) and employer.

On Wednesday, March 29, 2000 11:18 AM, Ming-Yi.Hu at pils-us.com wrote:
>
 
(snipped) 

> Does anyone know any comparison between S-Plus and SAS for clinical trial
> studies? 

I do not know of anything that directly addresses this question.  But
several years ago an example came out of the University of Wisconsin (DeMets
and Fisher) about thoughtfully using a few graphs produced by S-PLUS to
replace the voluminous tables format that is typically used to report data
analysis / statistical results to the FDA or Data and Safety Monitoring
Communities. See
http://www.splus.mathsoft.com/splus/whitepapers/drgapprv.htm .

> What are the advantages and disadvantages of using S-Plus over SAS in
> terms of analysis reporting and FDA submission? 

Start with disadvantages: The common belief is that the FDA only trusts
results in SAS and at worst will not accept results from any other analysis
software, and at "best" would raise questions that might slow down the drug
approval process.  This is a myth that unfortunately continues to be
perpetuated -- the FDA does not / can not endorse or require any software. I
believe S-PLUS output has shown up more frequently in recent years for
filings, but the challenge remains and the effort needs to be continued and
intensified. For advantages, see the previous and next responses.

> Does S-Plus 2000 have any new
> feature superior to SAS?
>
> Where can I learn these issues?

The short answer is: YES, many, and reference to version number (2000, 4.5,
5.1) is in some sense irrelevant.  S-PLUS has always had features superior
to SAS, from fundamental language to functionality, and particularly for
construction of data graphs.  SAS has always been stronger from an
efficiency standpoint when it comes to managing data sets.  These are but 2
of several issues to consider.  From my perspective, I have not used or
needed SAS in my work for nearly 3 years now, and the choice of methodology
when I analyze data is entirely my responsibility.  I see data sets from 10
observations to 100,000, and S-PLUS is fast enough.  I use a wide range of
statistical and data analytic methods, and I do not foresee ever having to
use SAS again.

There are other discussions of using SAS and S-PLUS on the Mathsoft Web
site; see http://www.splus.mathsoft.com/splus/pharm/comparison.htm .  The
Harrell reference there is more directly accessible at
http://hesweb1.med.virginia.edu/biostat/s/doc/splus.pdf, page 10-17.
 
> 
> Thanks,
> Ming-Yi Hu
> 

Best Regards,
Bill

