Subject: RE: [S] S+ vs. SAS
Message-ID: <852568B2.004F15B4.00@baltmta01.ms.bd.com>

From: Max Kuhn@BDX on 03/29/2000 04:23 PM

My beliefs are these:

Advantages of SAS:

It's better for rote calculation. From pharmaceutical trials, it's been my
experience that some regulatory agencies will want to see the most basic
analysis performed for every possible subgroup  (this opinion does not reflect
the opinions of Dr. Kuhn's employers....). In this case, "By processing" will
save your life. If you have to compute summary statistics for a large number of
subjects and then for each clinically significant subgroup, it can be quite a
task to do the calculations and organize them for everyone to easily process.

With version 7 and above, the ability to export results to html is very simple
and appears to be robust. The ability to get results out of S+ in report ready
formats, in my experience, are less mature than those in SAS are.

If any problems arise, SAS's technical support is timely and effective. On one
occasion, within 5 minutes of sending an email to tech support, my phone rang
with an answer. They we so efficient, it was spooky. If you are in the 30
response for a submission and you can't figure out why X is happening, this is a
blessing.

Advantages of S-Plus:

For many types of analyses, S-Plus will be better. In survival analysis they are
about equal, but any type of smoothing, bootstrapping or robust regression will
be simpler is S-Plus (there are other examples).

S-Plus graphics are superior. You can probably get SAS/Graph to do exactly the
same thing, but it will take considerable more time and effort. I've never had
problems exporting graphics in S-Plus and the selection of plots from the GUI is
substantial. I would much rather click on an axis to change it than to re-run a
portion of code after figuring out how to code it properly. However, if you are
creating a large number of graphs that are very similar, S-Plus can get bogged
down if you have too much open.

The bottom line:

You probably need both. It's not difficult to get data in and out of each. I
have found that I need these two packages plus Minitab (for SPC) plus StatXact
(for small sample studies) and so on, but I have a wide range of areas to work
in. For strictly clinical trials, I would leverage the strengths of both.

Max

